From 6c0f01c41cd4f503b15a78da97a574ceac3807f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 20:11:33 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 15/44] GE: share a depth buffer between the colour buffers
 that name it

Each render target had its own depth image, and the depth buffer address
a draw names (0x9E, 0x9F) was ignored. A game draws its scene into one
colour buffer and then shadow volumes into another, depth-tested against
the scene's depth buffer at the same address; those tests ran against an
empty depth image, so the stencil marked the wrong pixels.

Every picture of a depth buffer is now numbered by the target whose depth
image holds it. A draw that tests or writes a depth buffer whose newest
picture is in another target's depth image first copies that picture's
depth aspect into its own (the stencil stays: it belongs to the colour
buffer's alpha). Nothing is copied for a clear of the whole screen, when
the row lengths differ, or when the copy would overwrite the target's
own newest picture of another depth buffer. Depth images get transfer
usage for it. <prefix>_TRACE_FB_TEXTURES logs each copy;
<prefix>_NO_SHARED_DEPTH keeps every target's depth to itself, as before.

Verified on Windows (Vulkan, AMD) in one game's battle: one depth copy a
frame, and the shadow volumes now mark the floor around the fighters'
feet. Not verified: a game whose targets use one depth buffer with
different row lengths, or texturing from a depth buffer.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
